steps.reasoning:
We see repeated failures due to the date being overridden by getUnavailableDates after month change. The core issue is that when the user types a date that changes the month, the flatpickr's onMonthChange fires, causing getUnavailableDates to be called with the newly displayed month (the month of the date the user typed?), and inside getUnavailableDates, there is logic that if setDate is true (which it is), it will set the date to the first available day of the month. That overrides the user's typed date.

Our attempted fixes tried to restore the user's date after getUnavailableDates runs, but they didn't work because maybe the timing is off or the restoration is being overridden again.

Looking at the evidence: after typing "09/11/2026", the date field shows that value momentarily, then later becomes "01/11/2026". That suggests that after the user types, the date is correct, but then getUnavailableDates runs and sets it to the first available day (01/11/2026). So we need to prevent getUnavailableDates from changing the date when the user has just typed a valid date.

Idea: Instead of trying to restore after, we could temporarily disable the automatic date setting in getUnavailableDates when we know the user just typed a date. Or we could modify getUnavailableDates to not change the date if the user has recently typed.

But note: getUnavailableDates is also used for other purposes (like when navigating months via the picker UI). We don't want to break that.

Alternative approach: In the onMonthChange handler, we could compare the user-typed date with the displayed month moment and if they match (i.e., the user typed a date in that month), we could skip calling getUnavailableDates? But we still need to update unavailable dates for the new month.

Actually, the problem is that getUnavailableDates, when setDate is true, tries to set the date to the first available day of the month. That is undesirable if the user has already selected a date.

Maybe we can change the call to getUnavailableDates in onMonthChange to pass setDate:false when the change is due to user typing? However, the onMonthChange doesn't know if it's user typing or programmatic.

Alternatively, we can modify getUnavailableDates to not auto-set the date if the current date is already valid (i.e., not disabled). Look at the applyUnavailableDates function: it loops through days to find the first not disabled and sets it. We could skip that if the current date is already not disabled.

Let's examine applyUnavailableDates in booking_http_client.js:

```
function applyUnavailableDates(unavailableDates, selectedDateString, setDate) {
  setDate = setDate || false;

  $selectDate.parent().fadeTo(400, 1);

  processingUnavailableDates = true;

  // Select first enabled date.
  const selectedDateMoment = moment(selectedDateString);
  const selectedDate = selectedDateMoment.toDate();
  const numberOfDays = selectedDateMoment.daysInMonth();

  // If all the days are unavailable then hide the appointments hours.
  if (unavailableDates.length === numberOfDays) {
    $availableHours.text(lang('no_available_hours'));
  }

  // Grey out unavailable dates.
  $selectDate[0]._flatpickr.set(
    'disable',
    unavailableDates.map((unavailableDate) => new Date(unavailableDate + 'T00:00')),
  );

  if (setDate && !vars('manage_mode')) {
    for (let i = 1; i <= numberOfDays; i++) {
      const currentDate = new Date(selectedDate.getFullYear(), selectedDate.getMonth(), i);

      if (unavailableDates.indexOf(moment(currentDate).format('YYYY-MM-DD')) === -1) {
        App.Utils.UI.setDateTimePickerValue($selectDate, currentDate);
        getAvailableHours(moment(currentDate).format('YYYY-MM-DD'));
        break;
      }
    }
  }

  const dateQueryParam = App.Utils.Url.queryParam('date');

  if (dateQueryParam) {
    const dateQueryParamMoment = moment(dateQueryParam);

    if (
      dateQueryParamMoment.isValid() &&
      !unavailableDates.includes(dateQueryParam) &&
      dateQueryParamMoment.format('YYYY-MM') === selectedDateMoment.format('YYYY-MM')
    ) {
      App.Utils.UI.setDateTimePickerValue($selectDate, dateQueryParamMoment.toDate());
    }
  }

  searchedMonthStart = undefined;
  searchedMonthCounter = 0;
  processingUnavailableDates = false;
}
```

The problematic part is the block under `if (setDate && !vars('manage_mode'))`. It sets the date to the first available day of the month, overriding any existing date.

We want to avoid that if the user has already selected a date that is still available. So we could check if the current date (before this function) is still not disabled, and if so, skip setting.

But note: the function is called from getUnavailableDates with setDate:true (see line: `applyUnavailableDates(response, selectedDateString, true);`).

We could change that call to pass setDate:false when we want to preserve the user's date? However, we still need to update the unavailable dates display (grey out). The setDate flag also controls the auto-setting.

Thus, we could modify the caller to conditionally set setDate based on whether the user has typed a date.

But the caller is getUnavailableDates, which is used in multiple places: onMonthChange, onYearChange, initial load, etc.

We could instead modify applyUnavailableDates to be smarter: if setDate is true, but the current date is still valid (not disabled), then keep it.

Let's look at the current date before applying unavailable dates: we have selectedDateMoment from the parameter selectedDateString. That is the date that was passed to getUnavailableDates (which is the currently displayed month's first day? Actually, selectedDateString is the date string passed to getUnavailableDates, which in onMonthChange is the displayedMonthMoment.format('YYYY-MM-DD') (the first day of the displayed month). That is not the user's typed date.

